Hypothesis testing is a statistical method used to make inferences or draw conclusions about a population based on sample data. It primarily involves two key hypotheses: the null hypothesis (H₀) and the alternative hypothesis (H₁).

Mechanism of Hypothesis Testing:

Formulate Hypotheses:

Null Hypothesis (H₀): This is the initial assumption that there is no effect or difference, essentially stating that any observed effect is due to sampling variability.
Alternative Hypothesis (H₁): This represents the opposite claim of null hypothesis that suggests there is an effect or a difference.
Choose a Significance Level (α): Decide on a threshold for determining statistical significance, commonly set at 0.05.
experimental hypothesis: tell if null hypothesis is correct or not.

Select a Test: Depending on the data type and hypothesis, choose an appropriate statistical test such as:

Z-test: Used for assessing means when the sample size is large and population variance is known.
T-test: Used for assessing means when the sample size is small and population variance is unknown.
Chi-Square Test: Used for categorical data to evaluate how expected counts compare to observed counts.
ANOVA: Used to compare means across multiple groups.
Collect Data and Calculate Test Statistic: Use the selected test to analyze the sample data and compute the test statistic that will help determine the validity of H₀.

Determine P-value: This value indicates the probability of observing the data if the null hypothesis is true.

Make a Decision: Compare the P-value to the significance level: also caled alpha

there is a confident interval where the 95% of the chances goes and in that range we take hnull as true . the other 5% on both sides of the bell curve we take h null reject.

If P-value ≤ α, reject the null hypothesis (suggesting evidence for the alternative hypothesis).
If P-value > α, do not reject the null hypothesis (indicating insufficient evidence to support H₁).
Draw Conclusions: Summarize the findings and their statistical significance in the context of the research question.

In summary, hypothesis testing is a foundational concept in inferential statistics that allows analysts to draw conclusions about populations based on sample data, using various tests to evaluate different types of data and hypotheses.

